Read-only design consultation for the sefer-alloc Rust allocator. Do NOT edit any file, do NOT build, test, bench or run anything. Allowed: reading files, grep/glob, read-only git (log/show/diff). Forbidden: cargo build/test/bench/run/doc/clippy, miri, npm run check, any binary or script execution, any file write.

Read the code at worktrees/g1-k5 (branch g1-k5 = main + K1 + K5, the current best tree) and the measurement notes at worktrees/g1-pmu/docs/perf/G1_IDEAS.md (§0–§7, especially §1 catalog of rejected ideas, §2 lessons, §7 latest gap table) and docs/perf/G1_K5_DEPOT_GATE.md in the g1-k5 tree.

Goal G1: parity with mimalloc on the J1′ judge (real #[global_allocator], Windows, 11 kernels). Owner rules: protections against external input stay (double free, interior pointer, never-issued block, foreign pointer); a check may be removed only when provably safe; no writes into the body of the block being freed inside the dealloc call (R1-01, item 164 — see the comment in free_magazine_full, src/registry/heap_core/free/dealloc_own_base.rs).

Current state after K1+K5 (t_mimalloc/t_sefer, <1 = mimalloc faster): bulk16k 0.511, churn16 0.637, churn256 0.742, bulk64 0.783, churn64 0.786, pair16 0.821, churn1024 0.885, pair64 0.953, larson 0.981; vec512 1.29 and pair_rt 1.43 sefer faster. Judge kernels: examples/_shared/b4_interleave_kernels.rs (churn kernels share one 256-slot ring per arm, so churn16 frees blocks of other sizes left by other churn kernels).

Measured (callgrind, per op): alloc sefer ≈ 39 Ir vs mimalloc ≈ 36 (parity); free sefer ≈ 66 vs ≈ 21 on every kernel. bulk16k per alloc+free pair: free 74 vs 28, flush (magazine → per-segment class free list, writes next into block bodies + state byte) ≈ 47 + 7.6 full-magazine tail, drain (list → magazine) ≈ 27.5 + refill 5, alloc 40 vs 53. Time ≈ instructions (IPC ≈ 4.5 both), except where a change lengthens a dependency chain (K2: class from the state byte put a store→load on the critical path, pair16 +14 % cycles) or saturates the store buffer (GR-1: fewer flush instructions, slower). Probe A/B noise from code layout alone is ±5 % in cycles (K6, §7), so only effects well above that are visible outside the in-process ablation judge.

Free fast path (≈ 74 instructions): TLS + segment memo (OwnedBlock) 9, kind 3, class_for(layout) ≈ 10, payload_start / alignment / state byte / bump checks ≈ 11, magazine cap 3, stores ≈ 7 (state byte LIVE→MAG, slot, count), prologue/epilogue 6. Ablating any single step was worth ≤ 2 % (B4_ABLATE_CHURN, item 115).

Questions (answer in Russian, concise, with file:line references; separate measured facts from estimates):
1. Is a structural redesign of the own-thread free path warranted for G1, or is there a mid-size lever left inside the current design that the catalog (§1) has not already rejected? Name the single best candidate and its expected gain per kernel with reasoning.
2. Evaluate these structural directions for safety (owner rules above), RSS and expected gain: (a) per-segment class free list kept as an array of block offsets in segment metadata instead of next pointers in block bodies (flush/drain become copies of offsets, no body writes); (b) mimalloc-style per-page local free list for own-thread frees, with the magazine kept only as a front cache or removed; (c) a larger, budgeted depot (more K) for small classes so bulk shapes park magazines instead of flushing; (d) anything better you see.
3. For the recommended direction: the minimal prototype that would show the ceiling (what to change, where), the control shape that does not exercise the change (layout-noise floor), and the risks to invariants (state byte И1, double-free detection, remote frees, decommit/reset of segments, ghost-check rules in docs/verification/ghost-rules.json).
Final answer: a ranked recommendation with explicit reasoning; no code edits.
